  "generated_text": "To find the minimum value of the expression \\(\\frac{x^3 + 2y^3 + 4z^3}{xyz}\\), we start by applying the AM-GM (Arithmetic Mean-Geometric Mean) inequality. The AM-GM inequality states that for any non-negative real numbers \\(a, b, c\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{a + b + c}{3} \\geq \\sqrt[3]{abc}.\n\\]\n\nWe will use this inequality to bound the expression \\(\\frac{x^3 + 2y^3 + 4z^3}{xyz}\\). First, we rewrite the expression as:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{x^3 + 2y^3 + 4z^3}{xyz} = \\frac{x^3}{xyz} + \\frac{2y^3}{xyz} + \\frac{4z^3}{xyz} = \\frac{x^2}{yz} + \\frac{2y^2}{xz} + \\frac{4z^2}{xy}.\n\\]\n\nNow, we apply the AM-GM inequality to each term separately. For the first term \\(\\frac{x^2}{yz}\\), we have:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{x^2}{yz} = \\frac{x^2}{yz} + \\frac{yz}{yz} = \\frac{x^2}{yz} + \\frac{yz}{2yz} + \\frac{yz}{2yz} \\geq 3 \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{x^2}{yz} \\cdot \\frac{yz}{2yz} \\cdot \\frac{yz}{2yz}} = 3 \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{x^2}{4}} = \\frac{3x}{2\\sqrt[3]{2}}.\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, for the second term \\(\\frac{2y^2}{xz}\\), we have:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{2y^2}{xz} = \\frac{2y^2}{xz} + \\frac{xz}{xz} = \\frac{2y^2}{xz} + \\frac{xz}{2xz} + \\frac{xz}{2xz} \\geq 3 \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{2y^2}{xz} \\cdot \\frac{xz}{2xz} \\cdot \\frac{xz}{2xz}} = 3 \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{y^2}{4}} = \\frac{3y}{2\\sqrt[3]{2}}.\n\\]\n\nAnd for the third term \\(\\frac{4z^2}{xy}\\), we have:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{4z^2}{xy} = \\frac{4z^2}{xy} + \\frac{xy}{xy} = \\frac{4z^2}{xy} + \\frac{xy}{2xy} + \\frac{xy}{2xy} \\geq 3 \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{4z^2}{xy} \\cdot \\frac{xy}{2xy} \\cdot \\frac{xy}{2xy}} = 3 \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{z^2}{4}} = \\frac{3z}{2\\sqrt[3]{2}}.\n\\]\n\nAdding these three inequalities together, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{x^2}{yz} + \\frac{2y^2}{xz} + \\frac{4z^2}{xy} \\geq \\frac{3x}{2\\sqrt[3]{2}} + \\frac{3y}{2\\sqrt[3]{2}} + \\frac{3z}{2\\sqrt[3]{2}} = \\frac{3(x + y + z)}{2\\sqrt[3]{2}}.\n\\]\n\nTo find the minimum value, we need to check if equality can be achieved. Equality in the AM-GM inequality holds when all the terms are equal. So, we need:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{x^2}{yz} = \\frac{2y^2}{xz} = \\frac{4z^2}{xy}.\n\\]\n\nFrom \\(\\frac{x^2}{yz} = \\frac{2y^2}{xz}\\), we get:\n\n\\[\nx^2 xz = 2y^2 yz \\implies x^3 z = 2y^3 z \\implies x^3 = 2y^3 \\implies x = y\\sqrt[3]{2}.\n\\]\n\nFrom \\(\\frac{2y^2}{xz} = \\frac{4z^2}{xy}\\), we get:\n\n\\[\n2y^2 xy = 4z^2 xz \\implies 2y^3 x = 4z^3 x \\implies y^3 = 2z^3 \\implies y = z\\sqrt[3]{2}.\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(x = y\\sqrt[3]{2}\\) and \\(y = z\\sqrt[3]{2}\\) into \\(x = y\\sqrt[3]{2}\\), we get:\n\n\\[\nx = (z\\sqrt[3]{2})\\sqrt[3]{2} = z \\cdot \\sqrt[3]{4} \\implies z = \\frac{x}{\\sqrt[3]{4}}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(y = z\\sqrt[3]{2}\\), we have:\n\n\\[\ny = \\frac{x}{\\sqrt[3]{4}} \\cdot \\sqrt[3]{2} = \\frac{x}{\\sqrt[3]{2}}.\n\\]\n\nNow, substituting \\(x = y\\sqrt[3]{2}\\) and \\(y = z\\sqrt[3]{2}\\) into the expression, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{x^3 + 2y^3 + 4z^3}{xyz} = \\frac{(y\\sqrt[3]{2})^3 + 2(y\\sqrt[3]{2})^3 + 4(z\\sqrt[3]{2})^3}{(y\\sqrt[3]{2})yz} = \\frac{y^3 \\cdot 2 + 2y^3 \\cdot 2 + 4z^3 \\cdot 2}{y^2 z \\cdot \\sqrt[3]{2}} = \\frac{y^3 (2 + 4) + 4z^3 \\cdot 2}{y^2 z \\cdot \\sqrt[3]{2}} = \\frac{6y^3 + 8z^3}{y^2 z \\cdot \\sqrt[3]{2}}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(y = z\\sqrt[3]{2}\\), we have \\(z = \\frac{y}{\\sqrt[3]{2}}\\), so:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{6y^3 + 8\\left(\\frac{y}{\\sqrt[3]{2}}\\right)^3}{y^2 \\left(\\frac{y}{\\sqrt[3]{2}}\\right) \\cdot \\sqrt[3]{2}} = \\frac{6y^3 + 8 \\cdot \\frac{y^3}{\\sqrt[3]{8}}}{y^3} = \\frac{6y^3 + 8 \\cdot \\frac{y^3}{2}}{y^3} = \\frac{6y^3 + 4y^3}{y^3} = \\frac{10y^3}{y^3} = 10.\n\\]\n\nThus, the minimum value is \\(\\boxed{10}\\).",
